{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014DtMe6rvcQqyUUSfnti1kr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/049 `group_limited_topk_routing` (A800 / sm_80, Triton)\n2\t\n3\tRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-049_group_limited_topk_routing`\n4\tTarget: NVIDIA A800 (Ampere, sm_80). Primary implementation: **Triton** (PyTorch only for\n5\tmetadata/launch plumbing). No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n6\t\n7\tThis document is analysis-only. No `plan.md`, no solution code is produced in this step.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. Operation analysis\n12\t\n13\t### 1.1 Signature (from `task/definition.json`)\n14\t\n15\tEntry point `run(hidden_states, weight, expert_bias, routed_scaling_factor)`:\n16\t\n17\t| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n18\t|---|---|---|---|\n19\t| `hidden_states` | `[num_tokens, 4096]` | bf16 | token reps |\n20\t| `weight` | `[256, 4096]` | bf16 | gating/routing matrix |\n21\t| `expert_bias` | `[256]` | bf16 | per-expert routing bias |\n22\t| `routed_scaling_factor` | scalar | f32 | =2.5 in all feedback workloads |\n23\t\n24\tOutputs:\n25\t\n26\t| tensor | shape | dtype |\n27\t|---|---|---|\n28\t| `topk_idx` | `[num_tokens, 8]` | int64 |\n29\t| `topk_weight` | `[num_tokens, 8]` | float32 |\n30\t\n31\tFixed constants: `num_experts=256`, `top_k=8`, `n_group=8`, `topk_group=4`,\n32\t`experts_per_group=32`, `gating_dim=4096`. Only `num_tokens` varies.\n33\t\n34\tThis is the DeepSeek-V3 / Ring-flash \"noaux_tc\" group-limited routing (sigmoid gate + bias +\n35\t2-stage group-limited top-k).\n36\t\n37\t### 1.2 Reference math (the exact contract to reproduce)\n38\t\n39\t1. `logits = (hidden_states.float()) @ (weight.float()).T` → `[T, 256]` (fp32 matmul).\n40\t2. `scores = sigmoid(logits)` → `[T, 256]`.\n41\t3. `scores_routing = scores + expert_bias.float()` → `[T, 256]`. **Bias used for selection only.**\n42\t4. View `[T, 8, 32]`; within each group take **top-2** values and sum → `group_scores [T,8]`.\n43\t   (top-2 uses `sorted=False`; only the *sum* is used, so tie-order is irrelevant.)\n44\t5. Select **top-4** groups by `group_scores` (`sorted=False`) → `group_idx [T,4]`.\n45\t6. Build `group_mask [T,8]` (1.0 for selected groups), expand to expert level `[T,256]`.\n46\t7. `masked_scores = scores_routing.masked_fill(mask==0, float32.min)`.\n47\t8. Select **top-8** experts over the 256 masked scores (`sorted=False`) → `topk_idx [T,8]`.\n48\t9. `selected = gather(scores, topk_idx)` — **original sigmoid score, WITHOUT bias**.\n49\t10. `topk_weight = selected / (selected.sum(-1, keepdim) + 1e-20)`.\n50\t11. `topk_weight *= routed_scaling_factor`.\n51\t\n52\tTwo distinct score arrays must be tracked per token: `scores_routing` (sigmoid+bias) drives all\n53\tthree selections; `scores` (sigmoid only) supplies the returned weights.\n54\t\n55\tBecause the 4 selected groups contribute 4×32 = 128 candidate experts and we pick 8, there are\n56\talways ≥ top_k valid candidates → no degenerate/empty-selection case.\n57\t\n58\t### 1.3 Cost / roofline sketch\n59\t\n60\tDominant work is the gate GEMM `[T,4096] × [4096,256]`:\n61\t- FLOPs ≈ `T·256·4096·2`. For `T=6144`: ≈ 12.9 GFLOP.\n62\t- Memory: `hidden_states` = `T·4096·2` B (50 MB @ T=6144), `weight` = 2 MB (fits A800's ~40 MB\n63\t  L2 → reused across all token tiles), `logits` intermediate = `T·256·4` (6 MB @ T=6144).\n64\t\n65\tPer-token routing is 256-wide reductions → negligible vs. the GEMM.\n66\t\n67\tThe reference path launches the GEMM **plus** ~10 elementwise/topk/scatter/gather kernels over\n68\t`[T,256]` tensors, each a separate launch and each re-reading/writing `[T,256]`. The optimization\n69\tthesis is: **fuse the GEMM epilogue with the entire routing pipeline** so `logits` is never\n70\twritten to DRAM and the routing reductions happen in-register right after the K-loop, collapsing\n71\t~11 launches into 1 and removing several `[T,256]` DRAM round-trips.\n72\t\n73\t---\n74\t\n75\t## 2. Constraints & environment\n76\t\n77\t- Triton must own the computation; PyTorch only for shapes/dtype/stride/launch. No fallback of any\n78\t  kind (a failed Triton kernel is invalid, not something to paper over).\n79\t- Hardware A800 sm_80: bf16 tensor cores (HMMA) and TF32 tensor cores available; no fp8, no\n80\t  tcgen05/TMEM (those are Hopper/Blackwell). `KernelWiki` (SM90/SM100) and `ncu-report-skill`\n81\t  (B200/sm_100) are **out of scope** for this Ampere target, and profiling is prohibited, so they\n82\t  will not be used.\n83\t- Evaluation only through `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. I may **not** run CUDA,\n84\t  a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, torch directly, or any alternate correctness harness. Therefore every\n85\t  correctness/perf signal costs exactly one of the 100 candidate evaluations — reasoning must\n86\t  front-load correctness before each eval.\n87\t- Five fixed feedback workloads = one candidate evaluation. `num_tokens ∈ {2240, 2272, 6144, 2048,\n88\t  2112}`, all with `routed_scaling_factor=2.5`, random inputs.\n89\t- Budget: 100 evals; tokens soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Final 16-workload eval is operator-approved only.\n90\t- Immutable candidates: `c001, c002, …`; never mutate an evaluated ID; append-only `candidates.jsonl`.\n91\t\n92\t### 2.1 Tolerance interpretation\n93\t\n94\tPer-workload tolerances: `max_atol ≈ 0.56–0.65`, `max_rtol = 0.01`, `required_match_ratio = 0.98`.\n95\t`topk_weight` lies in roughly `[0, 2.5]` (normalized-to-1 weights × 2.5; mean selected weight\n96\t≈ 2.5/8 ≈ 0.31). An `atol ≈ 0.6` is ~2× a typical weight — deliberately loose. Interpretation:\n97\tnear-tie experts/groups can be selected differently by a faithful-but-not-bit-identical\n98\timplementation, changing a few weights substantially; the 0.98 match ratio tolerates ≤ 2% such\n99\telements. The unknown exact comparison (likely a set/order-insensitive or dense-scatter comparison,\n100\tsince `sorted=False` makes positional index order meaningless) means: **reproduce the reference\n101\t*set* of selected experts and their weights**; do not rely on any particular ordering of the 8\n102\toutputs. This is the single most important correctness design constraint.\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## 3. Numerical risks\n107\t\n108\t1. **Selection sensitivity near boundaries.** `sigmoid(logit)+bias` decides which group is 4th and\n109\t   which expert is 8th. Tiny logit perturbations flip borderline picks. The loose tolerance exists\n110\t   precisely because of this; still, minimizing GEMM error maximizes match ratio.\n111\t\n112\t2. **GEMM precision — and why bf16 tensor cores are actually faithful here.** Reference upcasts\n113\t   bf16→fp32 then does an fp32 `F.linear` over the *exact bf16 values*. Key observation: the inputs\n114\t   already carry only 8 mantissa bits (bf16). A bf16 tensor-core matmul multiplies these exact bf16\n115\t   operands and **accumulates in fp32**; each product of two 8-bit-mantissa numbers is exact in\n116\t   fp32, and the fp32 accumulator matches the reference's fp32 accumulation up to summation order.\n117\t   Hence bf16-HMMA-with-fp32-accum ≈ reference fp32 matmul (differences only from associativity of\n118\t   the 4096-term reduction, far below the 0.6 atol). TF32 (10-bit) is likewise lossless for the\n119\t   inputs. So the fast path (native bf16 tensor cores, fp32 accumulate) is *also* the numerically\n120\t   faithful path. Escalation ladder if a candidate shows correctness pressure:\n121\t   `tl.dot(bf16, acc=fp32)` → `input_precision=\"tf32\"` → `\"tf32x3\"` → `\"ieee\"` (true fp32, slowest).\n122\t   Whether the evaluator's reference ran with `allow_tf32` on/off is unknown; the bf16-input\n123\t   argument above makes all these variants agree, so I expect bf16-HMMA to pass.\n124\t\n125\t3. **Epilogue must be fp32.** sigmoid, bias add, top-2 sums, group scores, normalization, and the\n126\t   `1e-20` epsilon all in fp32 to match reference. Only the matmul multiplies use tensor cores;\n127\t   accumulation and everything downstream stays fp32.\n128\t\n129\t4. **Masking sentinel.** Reference uses `float32.min` (`-3.4e38`). For our in-register iterative\n130\t   argmax, using `-inf` (or a large negative) for both group-masked experts and\n131\t   already-selected-slot suppression is equivalent for max selection. Must ensure no NaN leaks into\n132\t   the normalization (selected values come from real sigmoid scores, never from the sentinel).\n133\t\n134\t5. **Tie-breaking vs. torch.topk.** For exact ties torch picks a specific index; iterative argmax\n135\t   picks by scan order. Exact fp ties on random data are measure-zero; within-group top-2 only needs\n136\t   the *sum* (tie-order irrelevant); group/expert selection sets (not orders) matter. Residual\n137\t   near-tie flips are absorbed by the 0.98 match ratio.\n138\t\n139\t6. **Summation order of the weight normalization.** `selected.sum` over 8 fp32 values — reproduce in\n140\t   fp32; ordering effect is ≪ atol.\n141\t\n142\t7. **Output dtypes.** `topk_idx` int64 (compute int32, store as int64), `topk_weight` fp32. Ensure\n143\t   correct casts.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t## 4. Triton design space\n148\t\n149\t### 4.1 Primary architecture — single fully-fused kernel\n150\t\n151\tGrid over token tiles: `program handles BLOCK_M tokens × all N=256 experts`. Because `N=256` is\n152\tsmall, one block can hold the entire expert dimension, so after the K-loop the block owns the full\n153\t`logits[BLOCK_M, 256]` and can run the *complete* routing epilogue in-register — no second kernel,\n154\tno `logits` DRAM round-trip.\n155\t\n156\tPer program:\n157\t1. fp32 accumulator `acc[BLOCK_M, 256]`.\n158\t2. K-loop over 4096 in steps `BLOCK_K`: load `hs[BLOCK_M,BLOCK_K]` (bf16), `w[256,BLOCK_K]` (bf16),\n159\t   `acc += tl.dot(hs, w.T, acc)` (bf16 inputs, fp32 accum). Weight tiles hit L2 (2 MB, reused).\n160\t3. `scores = sigmoid(acc)`; `scores_routing = scores + bias[None,:]` (fp32, bias broadcast over 256).\n161\t4. **Group top-2 sum** (8 groups × 32): per group, `m1 = max over 32`; mask out the argmax column;\n162\t   `m2 = max over 32`; `group_score = m1 + m2`. Implement over the `[BLOCK_M, 8, 32]` view via\n163\t   reshaped reductions (reduce over the size-32 axis). → `group_scores[BLOCK_M,8]`.\n164\t5. **Top-4 groups**: iterative argmax over the 8 group scores, 4 iterations, building\n165\t   `group_mask[BLOCK_M,8]` (set chosen to selected, suppress with `-inf` between iters).\n166\t6. Expand `group_mask` to expert level `[BLOCK_M,256]`; `masked = where(group selected,\n167\t   scores_routing, -inf)`.\n168\t7. **Top-8 experts**: iterative argmax over 256, 8 iterations. Each iter: `mval,midx = max/argmax`;\n169\t   record `topk_idx[:,i]=midx`; capture the companion **original** score via masked reduction\n170\t   `sel_i = sum(where(col==midx, scores, 0))` (avoids dynamic register indexing); suppress\n171\t   `masked[col==midx] = -inf`.\n172\t8. `denom = sum_i sel_i + 1e-20`; `topk_weight[:,i] = sel_i/denom * routed_scaling_factor`.\n173\t9. Store `topk_idx` (→int64) and `topk_weight` (fp32) with M-masking for the tail tile.\n174\t\n175\tCompanion-value trick (step 7) avoids a post-hoc gather into a register tile (Triton has no dynamic\n176\tindexing along a reduced axis); each of the 8 iterations costs a couple of 256-wide reductions —\n177\tnegligible vs. the GEMM.\n178\t\n179\t### 4.2 Register-pressure / tiling knobs\n180\t\n181\tThe `[BLOCK_M,256]` fp32 accumulator is the pressure point (256 KB RF / SM, 255 regs/thread).\n182\t- `BLOCK_M=16` → 4096 fp32 accum (÷128 threads ≈ 32/thread) — safe, high occupancy, many tiles\n183\t  (weight re-read from L2, cheap).\n184\t- `BLOCK_M=32/64` → larger tiles, better GEMM efficiency, more RF/possible spills.\n185\tSweep `BLOCK_M ∈ {16,32,64}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64,128}`, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.\n186\tAutotune is possible but adds compile cost/nondeterminism across the 5 workloads; prefer a small\n187\thand-picked config set chosen per-candidate to keep evaluations interpretable.\n188\t\n189\t### 4.3 Alternative — two-kernel split (fallback)\n190\t\n191\tKernel A: tuned GEMM → `logits[T,256]` in DRAM (6 MB). Kernel B: per-token routing reading `logits`.\n192\tPros: each kernel independently tunable, lower register pressure (no fused mega-accumulator), can\n193\treuse a well-shaped GEMM config. Cons: extra 6 MB write + 6 MB read (~a few µs) and a second launch;\n194\tloses the core fusion win. Keep as fallback if the fused kernel is occupancy-limited by the\n195\t`[BLOCK_M,256]` accumulator or if the epilogue inflates register usage enough to cripple the GEMM.\n196\t\n197\t### 4.4 Epilogue micro-optimizations (later candidates)\n198\t\n199\t- Fuse the group top-2 with the group-mask expansion to avoid re-materializing.\n200\t- Since `topk_group=4` of `n_group=8`, the group selection is tiny — an explicit\n201\t  4-iteration argmax over 8 is cheaper than a general sort.\n202\t- Represent group selection as a per-expert boolean without a full `[T,8]→[T,256]` broadcast where\n203\t  the compiler can fold it.\n204\t- Consider computing `group_scores` directly from a single pass that tracks running max1/max2 per\n205\t  group to halve the group reductions.\n206\t- Keep `scores` and `scores_routing` both resident (they differ only by the bias add) rather than\n207\t  recomputing sigmoid.\n208\t\n209\t### 4.5 Things to avoid\n210\t\n211\t- No `tl`-level general sort / full topk over 256 (expensive); iterative argmax with small k is\n212\t  cheaper and matches semantics.\n213\t- No fp16/bf16 in the epilogue reductions (precision + selection flips).\n214\t- No autotune configs that vary results across the 5 workloads in a way that muddies attribution.\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 5. Candidate roadmap (high level; concrete plan deferred to `plan.md`)\n219\t\n220\t- **c001** — Correctness-first fused kernel: bf16-HMMA fp32-accum GEMM + fp32 iterative-argmax\n221\t  routing epilogue, conservative `BLOCK_M=16/32`. Goal: pass all 5 workloads' correctness and\n222\t  establish baseline speedup. This is the highest-value, lowest-risk first shot.\n223\t- **c002+** — Performance tuning of `BLOCK_M/BLOCK_K/num_warps/num_stages` on the confirmed-correct\n224\t  kernel; one knob-set change per candidate for clean attribution.\n225\t- **cNNN (contingency)** — precision escalation (tf32/tf32x3/ieee) *only if* a candidate shows a\n226\t  match-ratio miss; two-kernel split if fused occupancy is the bottleneck; epilogue reduction\n227\t  fusions once GEMM tiling is settled.\n228\t- Stop when speedup converges or budget nears; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n229\t  without operator approval.\n230\t\n231\t---\n232\t\n233\t## 6. Validation strategy\n234\t\n235\tBecause no local torch/CUDA/profiler execution is permitted, validation is **reason-then-measure**:\n236\t\n237\t1. **Static faithfulness proof before each eval.** Re-derive each kernel step against §1.2 and §3\n238\t   (two score arrays; bias-in-selection-only; fp32 epilogue; sentinel handling; dtype casts; tail\n239\t   masking). Only submit a candidate whose math I can defend line-by-line against the reference.\n240\t2. **bf16-HMMA-faithfulness argument (§3.2)** justifies expecting correctness on the fast path;\n241\t   keep the precision-escalation ladder ready if the evaluator reports a match-ratio shortfall.\n242\t3. **One evaluation per immutable candidate** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n243\t   Read per-workload pass/fail + speedup; record parent, source hash, hypothesis, per-workload\n244\t   result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n245\t4. **Isolate variables.** Correctness-affecting changes (precision, selection logic) and\n246\t   perf-only changes (tiling) go in separate candidates so a regression is attributable.\n247\t5. **Correctness gate before speed.** A candidate that fails any workload's correctness is discarded\n248\t   regardless of speed; ranking is geomean speedup *among fully-correct* candidates.\n249\t6. **Budget discipline.** Front-load reasoning to minimize evals; prefer a curated handful of tiling\n250\t   configs over broad autotune sweeps; converge and stop rather than chase noise.\n251\t\n252\t### Open questions to resolve empirically (cheaply, via c001 feedback)\n253\t- Does bf16-HMMA meet the 0.98 match ratio on all 5 workloads? (Expected yes per §3.2.)\n254\t- Actual speedup magnitude of fusion vs. the multi-launch reference.\n255\t- Whether the `[BLOCK_M,256]` fp32 accumulator spills at `BLOCK_M=32/64` (inferred from relative\n256\t  speedups across tiling candidates, since direct profiling is disallowed).\n257\t\n258\t---\n259\t\n260\t## 7. Skill usage note\n261\t\n262\t`KernelWiki` and `ncu-report-skill` target Hopper/Blackwell (SM90/SM100) and B200 profiling; this\n263\ttask is Ampere (sm_80) and profiling is prohibited. Neither is applicable, so neither is invoked.\n264\tThis will be recorded as `skills: none` in the candidate records.\n265\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1f92ccf-4f21-4b34-84a4-e4e371081372","uuid":"01424e84-43bc-4d29-9b45-552ac2832341","timestamp":"2026-09-16T18:11:00.755Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-049_group_limited_topk_routing/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/049 `group_limited_topk_routing` (A800 / sm_80, Triton)\n\nRun ID: `formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-049_group_limited_topk_routing`\nTarget: NVIDIA A800 (Ampere, sm_80). Primary implementation: **Triton** (PyTorch only for\nmetadata/launch plumbing). No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n\nThis document is analysis-only. No `plan.md`, no solution code is produced in this step.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation analysis\n\n### 1.1 Signature (from `task/definition.json`)\n\nEntry point `run(hidden_states, weight, expert_bias, routed_scaling_factor)`:\n\n| tensor | shape | dtype | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `hidden_states` | `[num_tokens, 4096]` | bf16 | token reps |\n| `weight` | `[256, 4096]` | bf16 | gating/routing matrix |\n| `expert_bias` | `[256]` | bf16 | per-expert routing bias |\n| `routed_scaling_factor` | scalar | f32 | =2.5 in all feedback workloads |\n\nOutputs:\n\n| tensor | shape | dtype |\n|---|---|---|\n| `topk_idx` | `[num_tokens, 8]` | int64 |\n| `topk_weight` | `[num_tokens, 8]` | float32 |\n\nFixed constants: `num_experts=256`, `top_k=8`, `n_group=8`, `topk_group=4`,\n`experts_per_group=32`, `gating_dim=4096`. Only `num_tokens` varies.\n\nThis is the DeepSeek-V3 / Ring-flash \"noaux_tc\" group-limited routing (sigmoid gate + bias +\n2-stage group-limited top-k).\n\n### 1.2 Reference math (the exact contract to reproduce)\n\n1. `logits = (hidden_states.float()) @ (weight.float()).T` → `[T, 256]` (fp32 matmul).\n2. `scores = sigmoid(logits)` → `[T, 256]`.\n3. `scores_routing = scores + expert_bias.float()` → `[T, 256]`. **Bias used for selection only.**\n4. View `[T, 8, 32]`; within each group take **top-2** values and sum → `group_scores [T,8]`.\n   (top-2 uses `sorted=False`; only the *sum* is used, so tie-order is irrelevant.)\n5. Select **top-4** groups by `group_scores` (`sorted=False`) → `group_idx [T,4]`.\n6. Build `group_mask [T,8]` (1.0 for selected groups), expand to expert level `[T,256]`.\n7. `masked_scores = scores_routing.masked_fill(mask==0, float32.min)`.\n8. Select **top-8** experts over the 256 masked scores (`sorted=False`) → `topk_idx [T,8]`.\n9. `selected = gather(scores, topk_idx)` — **original sigmoid score, WITHOUT bias**.\n10. `topk_weight = selected / (selected.sum(-1, keepdim) + 1e-20)`.\n11. `topk_weight *= routed_scaling_factor`.\n\nTwo distinct score arrays must be tracked per token: `scores_routing` (sigmoid+bias) drives all\nthree selections; `scores` (sigmoid only) supplies the returned weights.\n\nBecause the 4 selected groups contribute 4×32 = 128 candidate experts and we pick 8, there are\nalways ≥ top_k valid candidates → no degenerate/empty-selection case.\n\n### 1.3 Cost / roofline sketch\n\nDominant work is the gate GEMM `[T,4096] × [4096,256]`:\n- FLOPs ≈ `T·256·4096·2`. For `T=6144`: ≈ 12.9 GFLOP.\n- Memory: `hidden_states` = `T·4096·2` B (50 MB @ T=6144), `weight` = 2 MB (fits A800's ~40 MB\n  L2 → reused across all token tiles), `logits` intermediate = `T·256·4` (6 MB @ T=6144).\n\nPer-token routing is 256-wide reductions → negligible vs. the GEMM.\n\nThe reference path launches the GEMM **plus** ~10 elementwise/topk/scatter/gather kernels over\n`[T,256]` tensors, each a separate launch and each re-reading/writing `[T,256]`. The optimization\nthesis is: **fuse the GEMM epilogue with the entire routing pipeline** so `logits` is never\nwritten to DRAM and the routing reductions happen in-register right after the K-loop, collapsing\n~11 launches into 1 and removing several `[T,256]` DRAM round-trips.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constraints & environment\n\n- Triton must own the computation; PyTorch only for shapes/dtype/stride/launch. No fallback of any\n  kind (a failed Triton kernel is invalid, not something to paper over).\n- Hardware A800 sm_80: bf16 tensor cores (HMMA) and TF32 tensor cores available; no fp8, no\n  tcgen05/TMEM (those are Hopper/Blackwell). `KernelWiki` (SM90/SM100) and `ncu-report-skill`\n  (B200/sm_100) are **out of scope** for this Ampere target, and profiling is prohibited, so they\n  will not be used.\n- Evaluation only through `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`. I may **not** run CUDA,\n  a profiler, `nvidia-smi`, torch directly, or any alternate correctness harness. Therefore every\n  correctness/perf signal costs exactly one of the 100 candidate evaluations — reasoning must\n  front-load correctness before each eval.\n- Five fixed feedback workloads = one candidate evaluation. `num_tokens ∈ {2240, 2272, 6144, 2048,\n  2112}`, all with `routed_scaling_factor=2.5`, random inputs.\n- Budget: 100 evals; tokens soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M. Final 16-workload eval is operator-approved only.\n- Immutable candidates: `c001, c002, …`; never mutate an evaluated ID; append-only `candidates.jsonl`.\n\n### 2.1 Tolerance interpretation\n\nPer-workload tolerances: `max_atol ≈ 0.56–0.65`, `max_rtol = 0.01`, `required_match_ratio = 0.98`.\n`topk_weight` lies in roughly `[0, 2.5]` (normalized-to-1 weights × 2.5; mean selected weight\n≈ 2.5/8 ≈ 0.31). An `atol ≈ 0.6` is ~2× a typical weight — deliberately loose. Interpretation:\nnear-tie experts/groups can be selected differently by a faithful-but-not-bit-identical\nimplementation, changing a few weights substantially; the 0.98 match ratio tolerates ≤ 2% such\nelements. The unknown exact comparison (likely a set/order-insensitive or dense-scatter comparison,\nsince `sorted=False` makes positional index order meaningless) means: **reproduce the reference\n*set* of selected experts and their weights**; do not rely on any particular ordering of the 8\noutputs. This is the single most important correctness design constraint.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Numerical risks\n\n1. **Selection sensitivity near boundaries.** `sigmoid(logit)+bias` decides which group is 4th and\n   which expert is 8th. Tiny logit perturbations flip borderline picks. The loose tolerance exists\n   precisely because of this; still, minimizing GEMM error maximizes match ratio.\n\n2. **GEMM precision — and why bf16 tensor cores are actually faithful here.** Reference upcasts\n   bf16→fp32 then does an fp32 `F.linear` over the *exact bf16 values*. Key observation: the inputs\n   already carry only 8 mantissa bits (bf16). A bf16 tensor-core matmul multiplies these exact bf16\n   operands and **accumulates in fp32**; each product of two 8-bit-mantissa numbers is exact in\n   fp32, and the fp32 accumulator matches the reference's fp32 accumulation up to summation order.\n   Hence bf16-HMMA-with-fp32-accum ≈ reference fp32 matmul (differences only from associativity of\n   the 4096-term reduction, far below the 0.6 atol). TF32 (10-bit) is likewise lossless for the\n   inputs. So the fast path (native bf16 tensor cores, fp32 accumulate) is *also* the numerically\n   faithful path. Escalation ladder if a candidate shows correctness pressure:\n   `tl.dot(bf16, acc=fp32)` → `input_precision=\"tf32\"` → `\"tf32x3\"` → `\"ieee\"` (true fp32, slowest).\n   Whether the evaluator's reference ran with `allow_tf32` on/off is unknown; the bf16-input\n   argument above makes all these variants agree, so I expect bf16-HMMA to pass.\n\n3. **Epilogue must be fp32.** sigmoid, bias add, top-2 sums, group scores, normalization, and the\n   `1e-20` epsilon all in fp32 to match reference. Only the matmul multiplies use tensor cores;\n   accumulation and everything downstream stays fp32.\n\n4. **Masking sentinel.** Reference uses `float32.min` (`-3.4e38`). For our in-register iterative\n   argmax, using `-inf` (or a large negative) for both group-masked experts and\n   already-selected-slot suppression is equivalent for max selection. Must ensure no NaN leaks into\n   the normalization (selected values come from real sigmoid scores, never from the sentinel).\n\n5. **Tie-breaking vs. torch.topk.** For exact ties torch picks a specific index; iterative argmax\n   picks by scan order. Exact fp ties on random data are measure-zero; within-group top-2 only needs\n   the *sum* (tie-order irrelevant); group/expert selection sets (not orders) matter. Residual\n   near-tie flips are absorbed by the 0.98 match ratio.\n\n6. **Summation order of the weight normalization.** `selected.sum` over 8 fp32 values — reproduce in\n   fp32; ordering effect is ≪ atol.\n\n7. **Output dtypes.** `topk_idx` int64 (compute int32, store as int64), `topk_weight` fp32. Ensure\n   correct casts.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\n### 4.1 Primary architecture — single fully-fused kernel\n\nGrid over token tiles: `program handles BLOCK_M tokens × all N=256 experts`. Because `N=256` is\nsmall, one block can hold the entire expert dimension, so after the K-loop the block owns the full\n`logits[BLOCK_M, 256]` and can run the *complete* routing epilogue in-register — no second kernel,\nno `logits` DRAM round-trip.\n\nPer program:\n1. fp32 accumulator `acc[BLOCK_M, 256]`.\n2. K-loop over 4096 in steps `BLOCK_K`: load `hs[BLOCK_M,BLOCK_K]` (bf16), `w[256,BLOCK_K]` (bf16),\n   `acc += tl.dot(hs, w.T, acc)` (bf16 inputs, fp32 accum). Weight tiles hit L2 (2 MB, reused).\n3. `scores = sigmoid(acc)`; `scores_routing = scores + bias[None,:]` (fp32, bias broadcast over 256).\n4. **Group top-2 sum** (8 groups × 32): per group, `m1 = max over 32`; mask out the argmax column;\n   `m2 = max over 32`; `group_score = m1 + m2`. Implement over the `[BLOCK_M, 8, 32]` view via\n   reshaped reductions (reduce over the size-32 axis). → `group_scores[BLOCK_M,8]`.\n5. **Top-4 groups**: iterative argmax over the 8 group scores, 4 iterations, building\n   `group_mask[BLOCK_M,8]` (set chosen to selected, suppress with `-inf` between iters).\n6. Expand `group_mask` to expert level `[BLOCK_M,256]`; `masked = where(group selected,\n   scores_routing, -inf)`.\n7. **Top-8 experts**: iterative argmax over 256, 8 iterations. Each iter: `mval,midx = max/argmax`;\n   record `topk_idx[:,i]=midx`; capture the companion **original** score via masked reduction\n   `sel_i = sum(where(col==midx, scores, 0))` (avoids dynamic register indexing); suppress\n   `masked[col==midx] = -inf`.\n8. `denom = sum_i sel_i + 1e-20`; `topk_weight[:,i] = sel_i/denom * routed_scaling_factor`.\n9. Store `topk_idx` (→int64) and `topk_weight` (fp32) with M-masking for the tail tile.\n\nCompanion-value trick (step 7) avoids a post-hoc gather into a register tile (Triton has no dynamic\nindexing along a reduced axis); each of the 8 iterations costs a couple of 256-wide reductions —\nnegligible vs. the GEMM.\n\n### 4.2 Register-pressure / tiling knobs\n\nThe `[BLOCK_M,256]` fp32 accumulator is the pressure point (256 KB RF / SM, 255 regs/thread).\n- `BLOCK_M=16` → 4096 fp32 accum (÷128 threads ≈ 32/thread) — safe, high occupancy, many tiles\n  (weight re-read from L2, cheap).\n- `BLOCK_M=32/64` → larger tiles, better GEMM efficiency, more RF/possible spills.\nSweep `BLOCK_M ∈ {16,32,64}`, `BLOCK_K ∈ {32,64,128}`, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`.\nAutotune is possible but adds compile cost/nondeterminism across the 5 workloads; prefer a small\nhand-picked config set chosen per-candidate to keep evaluations interpretable.\n\n### 4.3 Alternative — two-kernel split (fallback)\n\nKernel A: tuned GEMM → `logits[T,256]` in DRAM (6 MB). Kernel B: per-token routing reading `logits`.\nPros: each kernel independently tunable, lower register pressure (no fused mega-accumulator), can\nreuse a well-shaped GEMM config. Cons: extra 6 MB write + 6 MB read (~a few µs) and a second launch;\nloses the core fusion win. Keep as fallback if the fused kernel is occupancy-limited by the\n`[BLOCK_M,256]` accumulator or if the epilogue inflates register usage enough to cripple the GEMM.\n\n### 4.4 Epilogue micro-optimizations (later candidates)\n\n- Fuse the group top-2 with the group-mask expansion to avoid re-materializing.\n- Since `topk_group=4` of `n_group=8`, the group selection is tiny — an explicit\n  4-iteration argmax over 8 is cheaper than a general sort.\n- Represent group selection as a per-expert boolean without a full `[T,8]→[T,256]` broadcast where\n  the compiler can fold it.\n- Consider computing `group_scores` directly from a single pass that tracks running max1/max2 per\n  group to halve the group reductions.\n- Keep `scores` and `scores_routing` both resident (they differ only by the bias add) rather than\n  recomputing sigmoid.\n\n### 4.5 Things to avoid\n\n- No `tl`-level general sort / full topk over 256 (expensive); iterative argmax with small k is\n  cheaper and matches semantics.\n- No fp16/bf16 in the epilogue reductions (precision + selection flips).\n- No autotune configs that vary results across the 5 workloads in a way that muddies attribution.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Candidate roadmap (high level; concrete plan deferred to `plan.md`)\n\n- **c001** — Correctness-first fused kernel: bf16-HMMA fp32-accum GEMM + fp32 iterative-argmax\n  routing epilogue, conservative `BLOCK_M=16/32`. Goal: pass all 5 workloads' correctness and\n  establish baseline speedup. This is the highest-value, lowest-risk first shot.\n- **c002+** — Performance tuning of `BLOCK_M/BLOCK_K/num_warps/num_stages` on the confirmed-correct\n  kernel; one knob-set change per candidate for clean attribution.\n- **cNNN (contingency)** — precision escalation (tf32/tf32x3/ieee) *only if* a candidate shows a\n  match-ratio miss; two-kernel split if fused occupancy is the bottleneck; epilogue reduction\n  fusions once GEMM tiling is settled.\n- Stop when speedup converges or budget nears; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. Never run `final`\n  without operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation strategy\n\nBecause no local torch/CUDA/profiler execution is permitted, validation is **reason-then-measure**:\n\n1. **Static faithfulness proof before each eval.** Re-derive each kernel step against §1.2 and §3\n   (two score arrays; bias-in-selection-only; fp32 epilogue; sentinel handling; dtype casts; tail\n   masking). Only submit a candidate whose math I can defend line-by-line against the reference.\n2. **bf16-HMMA-faithfulness argument (§3.2)** justifies expecting correctness on the fast path;\n   keep the precision-escalation ladder ready if the evaluator reports a match-ratio shortfall.\n3. **One evaluation per immutable candidate** via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n   Read per-workload pass/fail + speedup; record parent, source hash, hypothesis, per-workload\n   result, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n4. **Isolate variables.** Correctness-affecting changes (precision, selection logic) and\n   perf-only changes (tiling) go in separate candidates so a regression is attributable.\n5. **Correctness gate before speed.** A candidate that fails any workload's correctness is discarded\n   regardless of speed; ranking is geomean speedup *among fully-correct* candidates.\n6. **Budget discipline.** Front-load reasoning to minimize evals; prefer a curated handful of tiling\n   configs over broad autotune sweeps; converge and stop rather than chase noise.\n\n### Open questions to resolve empirically (cheaply, via c001 feedback)\n- Does bf16-HMMA meet the 0.98 match ratio on all 5 workloads? (Expected yes per §3.2.)\n- Actual speedup magnitude of fusion vs. the multi-launch reference.\n- Whether the `[BLOCK_M,256]` fp32 accumulator spills at `BLOCK_M=32/64` (inferred from relative\n  speedups across tiling candidates, since direct profiling is disallowed).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Skill usage note\n\n`KernelWiki` and `ncu-report-skill` target Hopper/Blackwell (SM90/SM100) and B200 profiling; this\ntask is Ampere (sm_80) and profiling is prohibited. Neither is applicable, so neither is invoked.\nThis will be recorded as `skills: none` in the candidate records.\n","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
